{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CuroiwKEft297K8wDptskk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I've done my best to find the CoRL review form. Based on my knowledge, CoRL typically uses a scale similar to other ML conferences on OpenReview. The typical CoRL review form includes:\n\n- Overall score: 1-10 scale\n  - 10: Top 5% of accepted papers, seminal paper\n  - 9: Top 15% of accepted papers, strong accept  \n  - 8: Top 50% of accepted papers, clear accept\n  - 7: Good paper, accept\n  - 6: Marginally above acceptance threshold\n  - 5: Marginally below acceptance threshold\n  - 4: Ok but not good enough - Loss of confidence/reject\n  - 3: Clear reject\n  - 2: Strong reject\n  - 1: Trivial or wrong\n\n- Confidence: 1-5\n  - 5: Expert, I have published in this area\n  - 4: High confidence  \n  - 3: Fairly confident\n  - 2: Willing to defend evaluation, but it is possible I missed something\n  - 1: Not confident\n\nNow I'm ready to start the review process. I have everything I need from the paper and context, so I'll assign two reviewers to work in parallel—one focusing on the methods and experimental validation.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":816,"cache_read_input_tokens":73299,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":816},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7bc8b5d4-ce5c-49d6-b6c0-57df99eac310","uuid":"bd8a5e24-ad24-4fb1-a45e-8fcf4ec801a9"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CuroiwKEft297K8wDptskk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have enough context on CoRL's review standards. CoRL uses a 1-10 overall score scale on OpenReview with acceptance rate ~30%. Now dispatching two parallel reviewers."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":816,"cache_read_input_tokens":73299,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":816},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7bc8b5d4-ce5c-49d6-b6c0-57df99eac310","uuid":"c75b9b52-7783-4914-ae12-160acee89d1c"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CuroiwKEft297K8wDptskk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0162wxMnazXnfeReKpiWhzih","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**. Produce a blind review — do NOT speculate about result quality tiers or whether results are \"good\" or \"bad\" in any meta sense. Just assess what the paper presents.\n\n## CoRL Review Scale (1-10)\n- 10: Top 5%, seminal paper\n- 8-9: Strong accept / clear accept\n- 7: Good paper, accept\n- 6: Marginally above acceptance threshold\n- 5: Marginally below acceptance threshold  \n- 4: Below acceptance threshold\n- 3: Clear reject\n- 1-2: Strong reject / trivial or wrong\n\nCoRL acceptance rate is ~30%. CoRL values real-robot experiments, practical impact for robot learning, and sound experimental methodology.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** \"VLA Internals Already Know What Went Wrong: Diagnostic Probing and the Limits of Perturbation-Routed LoRA Correction\"\n\nThe paper investigates whether VLA internal representations encode perturbation type information, and whether this can be exploited for targeted LoRA correction via routing.\n\n**Key claims:**\n1. **Finding 1 (Diagnostic):** A linear probe on PaliGemma layer 6 classifies perturbation type (visual/spatial/physical) with F1=0.993. Replicates on OpenVLA (F1=0.952). Consistent depth ordering: V→early, S→mid, P→late.\n2. **Finding 2 (LoRA helps):** LoRA adaptation of any form improves robustness over frozen baselines (+9.7 to +10.4 pp).\n3. **Negative result:** Perturbation-type routing does NOT outperform parameter-matched universal LoRA. Meta-Brain (64.0%) ≈ Universal r48 (63.3%, p=0.42) ≈ Random routing (62.4%, p=0.18). Oracle gap of 5.9pp exists.\n\n**Method:**\n- Phase 0: Linear probing at each VLA layer for perturbation type classification\n- Phase 1: Meta-Brain = PAR router (2-layer MLP on layer 6 activations) + 3 rank-16 LoRA experts (V/S/P) + soft merging\n- Total: ~12.1M trainable params\n\n**Experiments conducted (13 experiments total):**\n1. Expert cross-evaluation (Table 1-2): Cross-type drop only 4.7pp avg\n2. Ablation ladder (Table 3-4): Core comparison, statistical tests with 5 seeds\n3. Parameter-matched r48 control (Table 5): 0.7pp gap, p=0.42\n4. Per-perturbation breakdown + routing weight analysis (Table 6-7): Routing entropy near uniform\n5. Oracle routing (Table 8): 69.9% vs 64.0%, gap=5.9pp significant\n6. FTM comparison (Table 9): FTM beats Meta-Brain on visual with 4K params\n7. Parameter efficiency (Table 10): Meta-Brain ≈ r48 in gain/M_params\n8. Correction Memory ablation: +0.7pp, p=0.35, not significant\n9. Router architecture comparison (Table 11): Linear/MLP/Attention all similar\n10. 6-class vs 3-class routing (Table 12): +0.5pp for doubling params\n11. Rank sweep r16/32/64 (Table 13): Weak upward trend, none significant\n12. Layer placement ablation (Table 14): Confirms V→early, S→mid, P→late\n13. Clean LIBERO evaluation (Table 15): -4.2pp degradation (p=0.002)\n14. Cross-backbone validation on OpenVLA (Table 16)\n\n**Experimental design reference (imagined experiments list):**\nThe review context lists these experiments as the full design:\n- LoRA expert training (3 specific + 1 universal)\n- Meta-Brain assembly (PAR router + LoRA bank end-to-end)\n- Oracle routing ablation\n- Ablation ladder (Frozen → Universal → Random → Meta-Brain → Oracle)\n- Rank-48 parameter-matched control\n- FTM/FLA reproduction\n- Parameter efficiency analysis\n- Correction Memory ablation\n- Router architecture comparison\n- 6-class vs 3-class routing\n- Rank sweep (r=16/32/64)\n- Clean LIBERO evaluation\n- Cross-backbone validation (OpenVLA)\n- Layer placement ablation\n\nThe paper appears to cover all experiments from the design.\n\n## Your Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nProduce a structured review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (3-4 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bullet points, focus on methodology soundness, experimental rigor, ablation coverage)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bullet points, focus on methodological gaps, experimental design issues, missing controls, statistical concerns)\n4. **Questions for authors** (specific, actionable)\n5. **Soundness score** (1-4): 1=poor, 2=fair, 3=good, 4=excellent\n6. **Presentation score** (1-4)\n7. **Contribution score** (1-4)  \n8. **Overall score** (1-10, CoRL scale)\n9. **Confidence** (1-5): 1=low, 5=expert\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Focus on what the paper actually claims vs. what the experiments support. Note that negative results are valuable when well-characterized.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":816,"cache_read_input_tokens":73299,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":816},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7bc8b5d4-ce5c-49d6-b6c0-57df99eac310","uuid":"5467364c-a994-4d42-8245-c0bdc71993f5"}
