s) + 3 rank-16 LoRA experts (V/S/P) + soft merging\n- Total: ~12.1M trainable params\n\n**Experiments conducted (13 experiments total):**\n1. Expert cross-evaluation (Table 1-2): Cross-type drop only 4.7pp avg\n2. Ablation ladder (Table 3-4): Core comparison, statistical tests with 5 seeds\n3. Parameter-matched r48 control (Table 5): 0.7pp gap, p=0.42\n4. Per-perturbation breakdown + routing weight analysis (Table 6-7): Routing entropy near uniform\n5. Oracle routing (Table 8): 69.9% vs 64.0%, gap=5.9pp significant\n6. FTM comparison (Table 9): FTM beats Meta-Brain on visual with 4K params\n7. Parameter efficiency (Table 10): Meta-Brain ≈ r48 in gain/M_params\n8. Correction Memory ablation: +0.7pp, p=0.35, not significant\n9. Router architecture comparison (Table 11): Linear/MLP/Attention all similar\n10. 6-class vs 3-class routing (Table 12): +0.5pp for doubling params\n11. Rank sweep r16/32/64 (Table 13): Weak upward trend, none significant\n12. Layer placement ablation (Table 14): Confirms V→early, S→mid, P→late\n13. Clean LIBERO evaluation (Table 15): -4.2pp degradation (p=0.002)\n14. Cross-backbone validation on OpenVLA (Table 16)\n\n**Experimental design reference (imagined experiments list):**\nThe review context lists these experiments as the full design:\n- LoRA expert training (3 specific + 1 universal)\n- Meta-Brain assembly (PAR router + LoRA bank end-to-end)\n- Oracle routing ablation\n- Ablation ladder (Frozen → Universal → Random → Meta-Brain → Oracle)\n- Rank-48 parameter-matched control\n- FTM/FLA reproduction\n- Parameter efficiency analysis\n- Correction Memory ablation\n- Router architecture comparison\n- 6-class vs 3-class routing\n- Rank sweep (r=16/32/64)\n- Clean LIBERO evaluation\n- Cross-backbone validation (OpenVLA)\n- Layer placement ablation\n\nThe paper appears to cover all experiments from the design.\n\n## Your Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nProduce a structured review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (3-4 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bullet points, focus on methodology soundness, experimental rigor, ablation coverage)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bullet points, focus on methodological gaps, experimental design issues, missing controls, statistical concerns)\n4. **Questions for authors** (specific, actionable)\n5. **Soundness score** (1-4): 1=poor, 2=fair, 3=good, 4=excellent\n6. **Presentation score** (1-4)\n7. **Contribution score** (1-4)  \n8. **Overall score** (1-10, CoRL scale)\n9. **Confidence** (1-5): 1=low, 5=expert\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Focus on what the paper actually claims vs. what the experiments support. Note that negative results are valuable when well-characterized.\n","uuid":"5e52eb1b-ca83-4563-a3f7-4a6864a78173","session_id":"7bc8b5d4-ce5c-49d6-b6c0-57df99eac310"}
