{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016b1sBXh2dvXkD2mB5oiGrH","type":"tool_result","content":"1\ttier: median\n2\tnarrative_guidance: 'Routing helps but the margin over rank-48 universal is modest\n3\t  (~6pp). Advantage is clear on Visual and Spatial but Physical shows limited routing\n4\t  benefit. Headline: \"~72% with ~12M params, +5.6pp over parameter-matched universal.\"\n5\t  Reframe as \"routing is most beneficial for perturbation types requiring qualitatively\n6\t  different representation-level corrections.\" Physical perturbation is the weak link\n7\t  — LoRA-P underperforms because physical perturbations require action-space correction\n8\t  beyond representation-level adaptation. Rank sweep shows routing benefit depends\n9\t  on expert capacity (inflection at r≥32). Clean LIBERO shows minor degradation (-2pp).\n10\t  Oracle gap (5pp) suggests PAR router leaves room for improvement — discuss as future\n11\t  work. Paper is viable at CoRL with sharpened per-type framing and honest capacity\n12\t  analysis.\n13\t\n14\t  '\n15\timagined_experiments:\n16\t- name: LoRA expert training — 3 perturbation-specific + 1 universal baseline\n17\t  plan_id: plan_002\n18\t  description: 'Train LoRA-V/S/P (rank 16, Q/K/V/O on all 18 PaliGemma layers) on\n19\t    type-specific data plus universal LoRA (rank 16) on mixed data. Evaluate each\n20\t    expert in-type and cross-type. Visual experts trained with online augmentation\n21\t    on LIBERO demos; Spatial/Physical experts trained on oracle-collected LIBERO-Plus\n22\t    demos.\n23\t\n24\t    '\n25\t  expected_results:\n26\t    lora_v_on_visual: 75.5\n27\t    lora_v_on_spatial: 63.1\n28\t    lora_v_on_physical: 59.8\n29\t    lora_s_on_visual: 62.4\n30\t    lora_s_on_spatial: 69.2\n31\t    lora_s_on_physical: 58.3\n32\t    lora_p_on_visual: 57.6\n33\t    lora_p_on_spatial: 57.2\n34\t    lora_p_on_physical: 61.0\n35\t    universal_r16_on_visual: 65.8\n36\t    universal_r16_on_spatial: 60.2\n37\t    universal_r16_on_physical: 55.4\n38\t    universal_r16_overall: 62.1\n39\t    avg_cross_type_mismatch_drop_pp: 10.8\n40\t    lora_v_cross_drop: 13.1\n41\t    lora_s_cross_drop: 9.0\n42\t    lora_p_cross_drop: 3.6\n43\t    training_converged_epochs_v: 15\n44\t    training_converged_epochs_s: 22\n45\t    training_converged_epochs_p: 30\n46\t    action_mse_val_lora_v: 0.0031\n47\t    action_mse_val_lora_s: 0.0039\n48\t    action_mse_val_lora_p: 0.0054\n49\t    action_mse_val_universal: 0.0042\n50\t  result_rationale: 'LoRA experts learn partially distinct corrections — cross-type\n51\t    drop exists but is asymmetric across types. LoRA-V shows strongest specialization\n52\t    (cross-drop ~13pp) because visual augmentation training creates a tight distribution\n53\t    match: the expert learns to invert specific visual corruptions (brightness shifts,\n54\t    background swaps) that are absent in spatial/physical data, so applying LoRA-V\n55\t    to non-visual inputs actively degrades representations by \"correcting\" features\n56\t    that weren''t corrupted. LoRA-S shows moderate specialization (~9pp cross-drop):\n57\t    layout perturbations alter mid-level spatial relationships that require qualitatively\n58\t    different corrections from visual corruptions, but there is partial overlap because\n59\t    spatial changes also affect visual appearance. LoRA-P is the weak link: only ~3.6pp\n60\t    cross-drop because physical perturbations (robot initial state) primarily affect\n61\t    action trajectories, not visual representations. LoRA-P''s correction at the representation\n62\t    level is modest (61% on-type, barely above universal''s 55.4%) — the signal for\n63\t    physical correction is weak at PaliGemma layer 6 where representations encode\n64\t    visual/spatial features better than proprioceptive state. Training convergence\n65\t    reflects this: LoRA-V converges in 15 epochs (strong, clean signal), LoRA-P takes\n66\t    30 epochs with higher validation MSE (noisy, weak signal). The average cross-drop\n67\t    of ~10.8pp is in the median range — specialization exists but is less dramatic\n68\t    than best-case, driven mostly by Visual/Spatial divergence while Physical contributes\n69\t    little.\n70\t\n71\t    '\n72\t  tables:\n73\t  - title: Per-expert success rates (rows=expert, cols=evaluation perturbation type)\n74\t    content: '| Expert | Visual | Spatial | Physical | Avg Cross-type | Cross Δ |\n75\t\n76\t      |--------|--------|---------|----------|----------------|---------|\n77\t\n78\t      | LoRA-V | **75.5** | 63.1 | 59.8 | 61.5 | −13.1 |\n79\t\n80\t      | LoRA-S | 62.4 | **69.2** | 58.3 | 60.4 | −9.0 |\n81\t\n82\t      | LoRA-P | 57.6 | 57.2 | **61.0** | 57.4 | −3.6 |\n83\t\n84\t      | Universal r16 | 65.8 | 60.2 | 55.4 | — | — |\n85\t\n86\t      | Frozen pi0 | 57.8 | 51.6 | 47.2 | — | — |\n87\t\n88\t      '\n89\t  figures_description:\n90\t  - '3×3 heatmap of expert success rates: diagonal (matched) cells are brightest.\n91\t    LoRA-P row is nearly flat — minimal on-type advantage. LoRA-V row shows sharp\n92\t    contrast between on-type (bright) and off-type (dim).'\n93\t  - 'Training loss curves (4 subplots): LoRA-V converges fastest/smoothest; LoRA-P\n94\t    shows noisy plateau; LoRA-S intermediate; Universal converges to moderate loss.'\n95\t  analysis: 'Experts DO specialize, but unevenly. The asymmetry is the interesting\n96\t    finding: Visual and Spatial LoRA show strong type-specific corrections (cross-drops\n97\t    13pp and 9pp), while Physical LoRA shows minimal specialization (cross-drop 3.6pp).\n98\t    This maps to the mechanistic story: PaliGemma''s representation space strongly\n99\t    encodes visual and spatial information (it''s a vision-language model), but physical\n100\t    state (robot joint positions, force dynamics) is weakly represented. LoRA-P''s"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0162wxMnazXnfeReKpiWhzih","session_id":"7bc8b5d4-ce5c-49d6-b6c0-57df99eac310","uuid":"33b4ea56-e36f-48d3-a47c-e684b372de15","timestamp":"2026-05-15T18:27:39.180Z"}
