{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Gt4nYBsMYkhqeZaMgqJdt2","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## Review: \"VLA Internals Already Know What Went Wrong: Diagnostic Probing and the Limits of Perturbation-Routed LoRA Correction\"\n\n**Reviewer R2 — Focus: Novelty/Originality and Related Work/Positioning**\n\n---\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper asks whether Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models internally encode perturbation type information, and if so, whether routing to perturbation-specific LoRA experts can improve robustness. The authors demonstrate via linear probing that perturbation types are linearly separable in VLA hidden states with near-perfect accuracy (F1=0.993 on pi0), and that this encoding follows a depth ordering (visual early, semantic mid, physical late). However, the core engineering contribution — Meta-Brain routing to specialized LoRA adapters — fails to outperform a parameter-matched universal LoRA baseline, yielding the paper's central negative result. The paper reframes itself around the diagnostic finding and the oracle gap (5.9pp) as an open problem.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n- **Honest negative result with statistical rigor.** The paper does not bury the failure of its proposed system. The statistical testing (permutation tests, p-values reported throughout, Holm-corrected comparisons) is unusually thorough for a robotics paper. The community benefits from knowing that this intuitive approach does not work as expected.\n\n- **The diagnostic probing finding has genuine novelty.** While SAFE demonstrated that perturbation *presence* is detectable, extending to fine-grained *type* classification at single-layer, single-step granularity is a meaningful step. The depth ordering result (V→early, S→mid, P→late) is interpretable and could inform future architecture design for robust VLAs.\n\n- **Cross-backbone replication.** Demonstrating the diagnostic finding on both pi0 and OpenVLA strengthens the claim that this is a general property of VLA representations, not an artifact of one architecture.\n\n- **Exhaustive ablation space.** The paper leaves few obvious ablation stones unturned — router architecture, routing granularity, rank sweeps, layer placement, 6-class vs. 3-class, correction memory, clean-task degradation. This thoroughness is a strength for credibility.\n\n- **Clear framing of the oracle gap as an open problem.** The 5.9pp gap between oracle routing and universal adaptation (p=0.004) is a concrete, well-characterized signal that expert specialization has untapped potential, which provides a clear direction for future work.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **Limited novelty of the central negative result.** The failure of perturbation-type routing is arguably predictable from basic MoE/routing principles: when expert capacity is low (rank 48 split across experts) and perturbation types share substantial representational overlap, routing fragments capacity without sufficient specialization benefit. The paper acknowledges this but does not engage deeply enough with *why* this was expected to fail from a theoretical perspective. A reader versed in sparse MoE literature (e.g., Switch Transformer, GShard) would find the outcome unsurprising.\n\n- **Severely underdeveloped related work — only 10 references.** For a CoRL submission, this is far below the norm. Major gaps include:\n  - No citation of foundational MoE work (Shazeer et al., 2017; Fedus et al., 2022) despite the routing mechanism being the paper's core proposal.\n  - No engagement with the broader LoRA composition literature (LoRAHub, AdaLoRA, DoRA) which addresses when and why adapter routing helps or fails.\n  - No reference to the representation probing literature in NLP (Belinkov & Glass, 2019; Hewitt & Manning, 2019) which established the methodology the paper relies on.\n  - Missing discussion of concurrent VLA robustness efforts beyond the three cited (e.g., RT-2 robustness analyses, GR-1/GR-2 approaches, or Octo-family adaptation strategies).\n  - The depth ordering finding (V→early, S→mid, P→late) is not contextualized against known layer-wise representation structure in vision-language models (e.g., CKA analyses in CLIP, LLaVA probing studies).\n\n- **Positioning relative to VLA-GSE is insufficient.** VLA-GSE achieves 81.2% on the same benchmark suite (LIBERO-Plus) using MoE routing by capacity with a different backbone. The paper acknowledges this but does not adequately address why its approach achieves only 64.0%. The backbone difference (pi0-FAST vs. Qwen3-VL-4B) makes direct comparison difficult, but the paper should either run on a comparable backbone or provide a principled argument for why the 17pp gap is attributable to backbone vs. method differences.\n\n- **No real-robot experiments.** All results are in the LIBERO simulator. CoRL has increasingly emphasized real-robot validation, and the paper's diagnostic finding — that VLA representations encode perturbation type — would be substantially more impactful if demonstrated on physical robot data where perturbations are naturalistic rather than synthetically injected.\n\n- **The diagnostic contribution, while novel, is narrow in practical impact.** Knowing that perturbation types are linearly separable in VLA hidden states is scientifically interesting, but the paper does not demonstrate a downstream use case where this knowledge leads to improved robot performance. The one attempted use case (routing) fails. Without a successful application, the finding remains an observation.\n\n- **Contribution C3 (oracle gap characterization) is thin as a standalone contribution.** Reporting that an oracle outperforms a learned router is expected. The rank-sweep evidence for emerging specialization at higher capacity (mentioned but with no significant results) does not substantiate a clear path forward.\n\n- **Clean-task degradation (-4.2pp, p=0.002) is concerning and underexplored.** The paper reports that Meta-Brain degrades clean LIBERO performance but does not adequately investigate why or propose mitigations. This is a practical concern: a robustness method that harms nominal performance has limited deployability.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On the oracle gap:** Have you analyzed *which* perturbation types benefit most from oracle routing vs. universal adaptation? If the 5.9pp gap is driven primarily by one or two perturbation categories, this would significantly clarify the nature of the specialization opportunity.\n\n2. **On capacity fragmentation:** Your rank sweep shows routing benefit of 1.0-2.0pp across r16-r64, none significant. Have you tried asymmetric rank allocation (e.g., giving more capacity to perturbation types with higher oracle-vs-universal gaps) rather than equal rank per expert?\n\n3. **On related work:** Can you justify the choice to cite only 10 references? The probing methodology, MoE routing principles, and LoRA composition literature each have extensive prior work that directly informs interpretation of your results.\n\n4. **On the depth ordering:** The V→early, S→mid, P→late finding is interesting but presented without mechanistic explanation. Do you have hypotheses about *why* physical perturbations are encoded later? Is this consistent with known processing stages in the pi0 architecture?\n\n5. **On VLA-GSE comparison:** Have you attempted to run your diagnostic probing on the VLA-GSE architecture? If VLA-GSE's MoE routing succeeds where yours fails, understanding whether the representation structure differs would substantially strengthen the paper.\n\n6. **On clean degradation:** The -4.2pp drop on clean LIBERO suggests the router or experts are interfering with nominal behavior. Did you try a gating mechanism that can route to a \"no adaptation\" path when no perturbation is detected?\n\n---\n\n### 5. Scores\n\n| Criterion | Score | Justification |\n|-----------|-------|---------------|\n| **Soundness** | 3 (good) | Methodology is rigorous, statistical testing is thorough, and the negative result is presented honestly. The probing experiments are well-designed. Minor concern: clean-task degradation analysis is shallow. |\n| **Presentation** | 2 (fair) | The paper is data-rich (16 tables) but the related work is severely underdeveloped. The reframing from system paper to diagnostic/negative-result paper creates some structural awkwardness. No figures shown is unusual. |\n| **Contribution** | 2 (fair) | The diagnostic probing finding is novel but narrow. The negative result, while honestly reported, is not deeply surprising. The paper does not deliver a method that works, and the open problem it characterizes (oracle gap) is not sufficiently developed to constitute a major contribution. |\n| **Overall** | 4 | Below acceptance threshold. The paper presents competent experimental work and an honest negative result, but the combination of (a) limited novelty over prior work, (b) no practical method improvement, (c) severely sparse related work, (d) simulation-only evaluation, and (e) predictability of the negative result places it below the CoRL acceptance bar. The diagnostic finding alone is better suited as a workshop paper or a component of a larger study that leverages the finding for a successful downstream application. |\n| **Confidence** | 4 | I am familiar with the VLA robustness literature, MoE/routing methods, and representation probing methodology. I have not reproduced the experiments but the reported methodology is sufficiently detailed to assess. |\n\n---\n\n### Summary Assessment\n\nThe paper's strongest asset is its intellectual honesty — the authors clearly invested significant effort into making routing work, and when it did not, they pivoted to framing the diagnostic finding and negative result as contributions. I respect this approach. However, for CoRL acceptance, the paper needs either (a) a method that delivers practical improvement for robot learning, or (b) a diagnostic finding with demonstrated downstream utility. Currently it offers neither. The related work section at 10 references is inadequate for a top venue and leaves the paper poorly contextualized. The simulation-only evaluation further limits its fit for CoRL. I encourage the authors to either find a successful application of their diagnostic finding (perhaps informing training curriculum, data augmentation strategies, or architecture modifications) or substantially expand the analysis to position this as a definitive negative result paper with broader lessons for the MoE-for-robustness paradigm."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ab5d69be13a4f59e1 (use SendMessage with to: 'ab5d69be13a4f59e1' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 17799\ntool_uses: 0\nduration_ms: 63128</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7bc8b5d4-ce5c-49d6-b6c0-57df99eac310","uuid":"1a79f2a0-586e-446d-bcdd-7f2b227ddf5a","timestamp":"2026-05-15T18:28:15.910Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Novelty/Originality** and **Related Work / Positioning**. Produce a blind review — do NOT speculate about result quality tiers or whether results are \"good\" or \"bad\" in any meta sense. Just assess what the paper presents.\n\n## CoRL Review Scale (1-10)\n- 10: Top 5%, seminal paper\n- 8-9: Strong accept / clear accept\n- 7: Good paper, accept\n- 6: Marginally above acceptance threshold\n- 5: Marginally below acceptance threshold  \n- 4: Below acceptance threshold\n- 3: Clear reject\n- 1-2: Strong reject / trivial or wrong\n\nCoRL acceptance rate is ~30%. CoRL values real-robot experiments, practical impact for robot learning, and sound experimental methodology.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** \"VLA Internals Already Know What Went Wrong: Diagnostic Probing and the Limits of Perturbation-Routed LoRA Correction\"\n\nThe paper investigates whether VLA internal representations encode perturbation type information, and whether this can be exploited for targeted LoRA correction via routing.\n\n**Three stated contributions:**\n1. A mechanistic finding that VLA internal representations encode perturbation type at single-layer, single-step granularity, with depth ordering (V→early, S→mid, P→late), replicated across two VLA architectures (pi0 and OpenVLA).\n2. A comprehensive ablation study establishing that perturbation-type routing does NOT outperform parameter-matched universal adaptation — presented as an honest negative result with statistical rigor.\n3. Characterization of the oracle gap (5.9pp) as an open problem, with rank-sweep evidence suggesting expert specialization may emerge at higher capacity.\n\n**Related work as presented in paper (Section 2):**\n- VLA robustness: FTM (visual-only, 71.2% on visual, 4K params), RobustVLA (uniform adversarial), VLA-GSE (MoE routing by capacity, 114M params, 81.2% on LIBERO-Plus with Qwen3-VL-4B backbone)\n- MoE and LoRA routing: MoIRA, CORAL (route by task identity), LoRA-SP (rank by complexity). Paper claims routing by perturbation type is orthogonal.\n- Representation probing: SAFE (perturbation presence detection). Paper extends to perturbation TYPE classification.\n\n**Key experimental results:**\n- Phase 0 probing: F1=0.993 (pi0), F1=0.952 (OpenVLA), linear separability confirmed\n- Meta-Brain (64.0%) ≈ Universal r48 (63.3%, p=0.42) ≈ Random routing (62.4%, p=0.18)\n- Oracle routing: 69.9% (gap=5.9pp, p=0.004)\n- FTM beats Meta-Brain on visual with 250× fewer params\n- Clean LIBERO degradation: -4.2pp (p=0.002)\n- Cross-backbone replication on OpenVLA confirms diagnostic finding generalizes\n- Correction Memory: +0.7pp, not significant, excluded from main system\n- Router architecture: Linear classifier (98.1% acc) achieves near-equivalent downstream (63.4% vs 64.0%)\n- 6-class vs 3-class: +0.5pp for 2× params\n- Rank sweep: Routing benefit 1.0-2.0pp across r16-r64, none significant\n- Layer placement: Confirms depth ordering but all-layer > subset by 3.8pp avg\n\n**Paper structure:** 10 pages, 16 tables, no figures explicitly shown (but described), 10 references.\n\n## Your Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nProduce a structured review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (3-4 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bullet points, focus on novelty of contributions, positioning relative to prior work, clarity of framing, significance of findings for the community)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bullet points, focus on novelty concerns, missing related work, positioning gaps, overclaiming or underclaiming, contribution significance relative to CoRL standards)\n4. **Questions for authors** (specific, actionable)\n5. **Soundness score** (1-4): 1=poor, 2=fair, 3=good, 4=excellent\n6. **Presentation score** (1-4)\n7. **Contribution score** (1-4)  \n8. **Overall score** (1-10, CoRL scale)\n9. **Confidence** (1-5): 1=low, 5=expert\n\nKey considerations for your focus:\n- Is the diagnostic probing finding (F1=0.993) novel given SAFE already showed perturbation PRESENCE detection? How much novelty does extending from presence to type add?\n- Is the negative result (routing doesn't help) a genuine contribution or a foreseeable outcome?\n- The paper has only 10 references — is this sufficient for a venue like CoRL?\n- VLA-GSE achieves 81.2% on LIBERO-Plus (different backbone) — how should this be positioned?\n- Is the paper's reframing from \"routing works\" to \"diagnostic tools + honest negative result\" a strength or weakness?\n- Does this paper have practical impact for the robot learning community?\n- ALL experiments are in simulation (LIBERO) — CoRL strongly values real robot experiments.\n","agentId":"ab5d69be13a4f59e1","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## Review: \"VLA Internals Already Know What Went Wrong: Diagnostic Probing and the Limits of Perturbation-Routed LoRA Correction\"\n\n**Reviewer R2 — Focus: Novelty/Originality and Related Work/Positioning**\n\n---\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper asks whether Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models internally encode perturbation type information, and if so, whether routing to perturbation-specific LoRA experts can improve robustness. The authors demonstrate via linear probing that perturbation types are linearly separable in VLA hidden states with near-perfect accuracy (F1=0.993 on pi0), and that this encoding follows a depth ordering (visual early, semantic mid, physical late). However, the core engineering contribution — Meta-Brain routing to specialized LoRA adapters — fails to outperform a parameter-matched universal LoRA baseline, yielding the paper's central negative result. The paper reframes itself around the diagnostic finding and the oracle gap (5.9pp) as an open problem.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n- **Honest negative result with statistical rigor.** The paper does not bury the failure of its proposed system. The statistical testing (permutation tests, p-values reported throughout, Holm-corrected comparisons) is unusually thorough for a robotics paper. The community benefits from knowing that this intuitive approach does not work as expected.\n\n- **The diagnostic probing finding has genuine novelty.** While SAFE demonstrated that perturbation *presence* is detectable, extending to fine-grained *type* classification at single-layer, single-step granularity is a meaningful step. The depth ordering result (V→early, S→mid, P→late) is interpretable and could inform future architecture design for robust VLAs.\n\n- **Cross-backbone replication.** Demonstrating the diagnostic finding on both pi0 and OpenVLA strengthens the claim that this is a general property of VLA representations, not an artifact of one architecture.\n\n- **Exhaustive ablation space.** The paper leaves few obvious ablation stones unturned — router architecture, routing granularity, rank sweeps, layer placement, 6-class vs. 3-class, correction memory, clean-task degradation. This thoroughness is a strength for credibility.\n\n- **Clear framing of the oracle gap as an open problem.** The 5.9pp gap between oracle routing and universal adaptation (p=0.004) is a concrete, well-characterized signal that expert specialization has untapped potential, which provides a clear direction for future work.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **Limited novelty of the central negative result.** The failure of perturbation-type routing is arguably predictable from basic MoE/routing principles: when expert capacity is low (rank 48 split across experts) and perturbation types share substantial representational overlap, routing fragments capacity without sufficient specialization benefit. The paper acknowledges this but does not engage deeply enough with *why* this was expected to fail from a theoretical perspective. A reader versed in sparse MoE literature (e.g., Switch Transformer, GShard) would find the outcome unsurprising.\n\n- **Severely underdeveloped related work — only 10 references.** For a CoRL submission, this is far below the norm. Major gaps include:\n  - No citation of foundational MoE work (Shazeer et al., 2017; Fedus et al., 2022) despite the routing mechanism being the paper's core proposal.\n  - No engagement with the broader LoRA composition literature (LoRAHub, AdaLoRA, DoRA) which addresses when and why adapter routing helps or fails.\n  - No reference to the representation probing literature in NLP (Belinkov & Glass, 2019; Hewitt & Manning, 2019) which established the methodology the paper relies on.\n  - Missing discussion of concurrent VLA robustness efforts beyond the three cited (e.g., RT-2 robustness analyses, GR-1/GR-2 approaches, or Octo-family adaptation strategies).\n  - The depth ordering finding (V→early, S→mid, P→late) is not contextualized against known layer-wise representation structure in vision-language models (e.g., CKA analyses in CLIP, LLaVA probing studies).\n\n- **Positioning relative to VLA-GSE is insufficient.** VLA-GSE achieves 81.2% on the same benchmark suite (LIBERO-Plus) using MoE routing by capacity with a different backbone. The paper acknowledges this but does not adequately address why its approach achieves only 64.0%. The backbone difference (pi0-FAST vs. Qwen3-VL-4B) makes direct comparison difficult, but the paper should either run on a comparable backbone or provide a principled argument for why the 17pp gap is attributable to backbone vs. method differences.\n\n- **No real-robot experiments.** All results are in the LIBERO simulator. CoRL has increasingly emphasized real-robot validation, and the paper's diagnostic finding — that VLA representations encode perturbation type — would be substantially more impactful if demonstrated on physical robot data where perturbations are naturalistic rather than synthetically injected.\n\n- **The diagnostic contribution, while novel, is narrow in practical impact.** Knowing that perturbation types are linearly separable in VLA hidden states is scientifically interesting, but the paper does not demonstrate a downstream use case where this knowledge leads to improved robot performance. The one attempted use case (routing) fails. Without a successful application, the finding remains an observation.\n\n- **Contribution C3 (oracle gap characterization) is thin as a standalone contribution.** Reporting that an oracle outperforms a learned router is expected. The rank-sweep evidence for emerging specialization at higher capacity (mentioned but with no significant results) does not substantiate a clear path forward.\n\n- **Clean-task degradation (-4.2pp, p=0.002) is concerning and underexplored.** The paper reports that Meta-Brain degrades clean LIBERO performance but does not adequately investigate why or propose mitigations. This is a practical concern: a robustness method that harms nominal performance has limited deployability.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On the oracle gap:** Have you analyzed *which* perturbation types benefit most from oracle routing vs. universal adaptation? If the 5.9pp gap is driven primarily by one or two perturbation categories, this would significantly clarify the nature of the specialization opportunity.\n\n2. **On capacity fragmentation:** Your rank sweep shows routing benefit of 1.0-2.0pp across r16-r64, none significant. Have you tried asymmetric rank allocation (e.g., giving more capacity to perturbation types with higher oracle-vs-universal gaps) rather than equal rank per expert?\n\n3. **On related work:** Can you justify the choice to cite only 10 references? The probing methodology, MoE routing principles, and LoRA composition literature each have extensive prior work that directly informs interpretation of your results.\n\n4. **On the depth ordering:** The V→early, S→mid, P→late finding is interesting but presented without mechanistic explanation. Do you have hypotheses about *why* physical perturbations are encoded later? Is this consistent with known processing stages in the pi0 architecture?\n\n5. **On VLA-GSE comparison:** Have you attempted to run your diagnostic probing on the VLA-GSE architecture? If VLA-GSE's MoE routing succeeds where yours fails, understanding whether the representation structure differs would substantially strengthen the paper.\n\n6. **On clean degradation:** The -4.2pp drop on clean LIBERO suggests the router or experts are interfering with nominal behavior. Did you try a gating mechanism that can route to a \"no adaptation\" path when no perturbation is detected?\n\n---\n\n### 5. Scores\n\n| Criterion | Score | Justification |\n|-----------|-------|---------------|\n| **Soundness** | 3 (good) | Methodology is rigorous, statistical testing is thorough, and the negative result is presented honestly. The probing experiments are well-designed. Minor concern: clean-task degradation analysis is shallow. |\n| **Presentation** | 2 (fair) | The paper is data-rich (16 tables) but the related work is severely underdeveloped. The reframing from system paper to diagnostic/negative-result paper creates some structural awkwardness. No figures shown is unusual. |\n| **Contribution** | 2 (fair) | The diagnostic probing finding is novel but narrow. The negative result, while honestly reported, is not deeply surprising. The paper does not deliver a method that works, and the open problem it characterizes (oracle gap) is not sufficiently developed to constitute a major contribution. |\n| **Overall** | 4 | Below acceptance threshold. The paper presents competent experimental work and an honest negative result, but the combination of (a) limited novelty over prior work, (b) no practical method improvement, (c) severely sparse related work, (d) simulation-only evaluation, and (e) predictability of the negative result places it below the CoRL acceptance bar. The diagnostic finding alone is better suited as a workshop paper or a component of a larger study that leverages the finding for a successful downstream application. |\n| **Confidence** | 4 | I am familiar with the VLA robustness literature, MoE/routing methods, and representation probing methodology. I have not reproduced the experiments but the reported methodology is sufficiently detailed to assess. |\n\n---\n\n### Summary Assessment\n\nThe paper's strongest asset is its intellectual honesty — the authors clearly invested significant effort into making routing work, and when it did not, they pivoted to framing the diagnostic finding and negative result as contributions. I respect this approach. However, for CoRL acceptance, the paper needs either (a) a method that delivers practical improvement for robot learning, or (b) a diagnostic finding with demonstrated downstream utility. Currently it offers neither. The related work section at 10 references is inadequate for a top venue and leaves the paper poorly contextualized. The simulation-only evaluation further limits its fit for CoRL. I encourage the authors to either find a successful application of their diagnostic finding (perhaps informing training curriculum, data augmentation strategies, or architecture modifications) or substantially expand the analysis to position this as a definitive negative result paper with broader lessons for the MoE-for-robustness paradigm."}],"totalDurationMs":63128,"totalTokens":17799,"totalToolUseCount":0,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7151,"cache_read_input_tokens":8252,"output_tokens":2394,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7151},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2394,"cache_read_input_tokens":8252,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7151,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7151,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}}}
